@title Orders platform architecture
@subtitle Commerce Platform · Kestrel Outfitters · last reviewed 2026-10-01

# Overview

The Orders platform takes web orders, reserves stock and charges cards. It handles about 3200 orders per hour at peak.

# Architecture diagram

diagram orders dir=LR title="Orders platform"
orders: cust "Customers" user
orders: stripe "Stripe" ext
orders: alb "Application Load Balancer" lb
orders: svc "Orders Service" svc
orders: inv "Inventory Service" svc
orders: db "Orders DB" db
orders: q "Order Events" queue
orders: ecr "ECR Registry" store
orders: group vpc "Orders VPC (10.20.0.0/16)" alb, svc, inv, db
orders: group prod "Prod account" vpc, q
orders: group shared "Shared services account" ecr
orders: cust -> alb : HTTPS
orders: alb -> svc : /orders
orders: alb -> inv : /inventory
orders: svc -> db : SQL
orders: svc -> q : publish
orders: q -> inv : poll
orders: svc -> stripe : charge
orders: ecr -> svc : image pull

# Components

table = Component,Runtime,Purpose; Orders Service,"ECS Fargate, 6 tasks",Takes orders and charges cards; Inventory Service,"ECS Fargate, 4 tasks",Reserves stock; Orders DB,"RDS PostgreSQL 16 on db.r6g.xlarge, Multi-AZ",Stores orders; Order Events,"Standard SQS queue, 14 day retention",Carries order events to Inventory

# Failure modes

!! An Orders DB Multi-AZ failover takes about 60 seconds. During that time, order writes fail and the storefront shows a retry banner.
